        <div class="expert">
          <article id="x-a1">
            <h3>setState() does not rebuild anything itself.</h3>
            <p class="sum">It runs your callback, marks the State's Element dirty, and the framework rebuilds that Element in the next frame. The rebuild flows down through reconciliation, never up.</p>
            <ol>
              <li><b>Trigger.</b> <code>State.setState(fn)</code> runs <code>fn</code> synchronously (it must not return a Future), then calls <code>Element.markNeedsBuild()</code>.</li>
              <li><b>Mark.</b> The Element is flagged dirty and added to the <code>BuildOwner</code> dirty list (<code>scheduleBuildFor</code>). An already dirty Element is not marked again, so extra calls in the same frame cost no extra build. The first dirty Element makes the framework request a frame (<code>onBuildScheduled</code>; in the source this leads to <code>ensureVisualUpdate</code> and <code>scheduleFrame</code>).</li>
              <li><b>Frame.</b> The engine calls <code>drawFrame</code>. Its order: build, layout, compositing bits, paint, compositing, semantics, then finalization (<code>State.dispose</code> for objects removed this frame).</li>
              <li><b>Build.</b> <code>buildScope</code> rebuilds the dirty Elements in depth order, parents before children. For a <code>StatefulElement</code>, <code>rebuild()</code> leads to <code>performRebuild()</code>, which calls <code>State.build()</code> and gets a new widget subtree.</li>
              <li><b>Reconcile.</b> <code>updateChild</code> compares each old child with its new widget. Same widget (<code>==</code>, for example <code>const</code>): skipped. Same <code>runtimeType</code> and <code>key</code> (<code>Widget.canUpdate</code>): the Element is reused and <code>update()</code> runs, which triggers <code>didUpdateWidget</code> on a State, then it rebuilds. Otherwise the old Element is deactivated and a new one is inflated.</li>
              <li><b>Render.</b> A <code>RenderObjectElement</code> copies the new configuration with <code>updateRenderObject</code>. <code>markNeedsLayout</code> and <code>markNeedsPaint</code> only mark work; it runs in that frame's layout and paint phases.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="end"><b>What rebuilds:</b> the dirty Element, plus the descendants that reconciliation reaches and does not skip. Ancestors and siblings are untouched. (A separate trigger: dependents of an <code>InheritedWidget</code> rebuild when it changes.)</p>
          </article>
          <article id="x-a2" hidden>
            <h3>Not the entire app. Not necessarily the whole screen.</h3>
            <p class="sum">Only the subtree under the State's Element. That is the whole screen only when the State sits at the top of the screen.</p>
            <h4>Why it stops there</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Only <b>one Element</b> is marked dirty, and <code>buildScope</code> starts from the dirty list, not from the root.</li>
              <li>Configuration flows <b>down</b>. A child cannot rebuild its parent.</li>
              <li>Reconciliation can stop inside the subtree: an identical widget (<code>const</code> or cached) is skipped with its whole branch.</li>
              <li>So keep <code>setState</code> low, use <code>const</code>, and prefer widget classes over helper methods (Flutter performance docs).</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>build() vs the Element being recreated</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><b><code>build()</code></b> is a State method that returns a new, immutable widget description. It can run many times: every <code>setState</code>, every parent rebuild that hands over a new widget, every dependency change. It is cheap.</li>
              <li><b>The Element</b> is the long-lived object in the tree. It holds the State and the link to the RenderObject. It is created once (<code>createElement</code>, <code>mount</code>) and reused while the widget at that spot keeps the same <code>runtimeType</code> and <code>key</code>.</li>
              <li>It is <b>recreated</b> only if the type or key at that spot changes, the widget is removed, or an ancestor Element is recreated. A <code>GlobalKey</code> can move a subtree if it arrives in the same frame.</li>
              <li><b>Lifecycle effect:</b> <code>createState</code> and <code>initState</code> run once per Element. <code>build</code> runs many times. <code>didUpdateWidget</code> runs when a parent supplies a new widget with the same type and key. <code>dispose</code> runs once, during frame finalization after removal.</li>
              <li><b>Pitfall:</b> unkeyed siblings are matched by type and position, so reordering can attach the wrong State. Use keys.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
